L both? No overlap.\n\n- Head nod inside bob group: `<g class=\"head-group\">` with head, beak, helmet, eye. CSS rotate around 600 210. Use transform-origin with `transform-box: view-box;`? CSS transform-origin for SVG g in user units: defaults to 0 0? Actually transform-origin default is 0 0 for SVG (transform-box view-box). We can set `transform-origin: 600px 230px;` and `transform-box: view-box;`. Modern supports transform-box: view-box. For broad compatibility, use `transform-origin: 600px 230px`. Might be okay. Alternatively use SMIL animateTransform for head nod around explicit coordinate. Safer. Use SMIL:\n```\n<g id=\"head\">\n <animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"rotate\" values=\"-1 590 225;1.5 590 225;-1 590 225\" dur=\"1.7s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" keyTimes=\"0;0.5;1\" calcMode=\"spline\" keySplines=\"...\"/>\n```\nBut parse. Use CSS. Better maybe body bob only, no head nod. Active legs/wheels/cranks sufficient. Could animate scarf and body.\n\nMaybe use CSS for bob/neck/scarf. Test in browser? rsvg ignores CSS animations but static fine. CSS transform-origin might be okay.\n\nWe can use SMIL for all key motion: wheel, crank, legs, scarf flutter, bob. SMIL supports group transform. But if we apply CSS and SMIL to different groups no conflict. Ease of animation. Let's mostly use SMIL for predictable. Yet body bob with animateTransform translate can be:\n`<animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"translate\" values=\"0 0;0 -3;0 0\" dur=\"1.7s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" calcMode=\"spline\" keyTimes=\"0;0.5;1\" keySplines=\"0.45 0 0.55 1;0.45 0 0.55 1\"/>`.\nThis works. Add to group. Scarf rotation with animateTransform values=\"-7 548 232;8 548 232;-7 548 232\" dur=\"0.9s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\" calcMode=\"spline\"... Add to scarf tail group. Need groups have static transform? At t=0, animation values apply. Static base if SMIL unsupported is unrotated. Fine.\nSpeed lines CSS easier. Could use SMIL with begin offsets. Use CSS for repetitive opacity and translation. However CSS animations with transform on SVG `<g>` support `translateX`. Set `transform-box: fill-box; transform-origin: center;` but translation in CSS px; works. Could also simply use SMIL:\n```\n<g>\n <animateTransform attributeName=\"transform\" type=\"translate\" from=\"0 0\" to=\"-60 0\" dur=\"1.8s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\"/>\n <animate attributeName=\"opacity\" values=\"0;0.55;0\" dur=\"1.8s\" repeatCount=\"indefinite\"/>\n <line .../>\n</g>\n```\nIf line group starts at x=50..170, translation -60 might go out. Fine. Use multiple lines with begin? SMIL begin can be negative to stagger: begin=\"-0.4s\". Good.\n\nRoad dashes: SMIL animate stroke-dashoffset from \"0\" to \"-78\" dur=\"1s\" repeat. Works. Great.\n\nCloud drift: animateTransform translate values \"0 0;-20 0;0 0\" dur=\"18s\" repeat. Fine.\n\nCSS can be minimal or none. Use SMIL for everything. Some renderers may not animate but file still looks good. SMIL is standard and supported. User likely views in browser.\n\nNeed add a motion blur? no.\n\nPotential issue with `<animate attributeName=\"d\" values=\"...;...;\" dur=...>`: In SVG, path endpoints and commands must be exactly same; yes. Browser may animate linearly. Good. Use `calcMode=\"linear\"`.\n\nNeed possibly specify `fill=\"freeze\"`? no repeat indefinite.\n\nLet's construct file. We'll iterate on visual after render.\n\nBackground specifics:\n\n```\n<rect width=\"1000\" height=\"700\" fill=\"url(#sky)\"/>\n<circle cx=\"845\" cy=\"130\" r=\"110\" fill=\"url(#sunGlow)\" opacity=\".55\"/>\n<circle cx=\"845\" cy=\"130\" r=\"58\" fill=\"#ffdf8f\"/>\n```\nMaybe sun is too close to beak; okay.\n\nClouds:\n```\n<g opacity=\".6\" fill=\"#fff\">\n  <animateTransform ...>\n  <ellipse cx=150 cy=125 rx=44 ry=18/>\n  <ellipse cx=185 cy=115 rx=34 ry=22/>\n...\n</g>\n```\nMaybe cloud shapes with path. Add 3 clouds.\n\nHills / coast:\n`<path d=\"M0 390 C 100 360 180 375 260 390 C 340 405 380 380 470 385 C 590 392 640 370 750 382 C 850 393 920 370 1000 380 L1000 470 L0 470 Z\" fill=\"#a9d6c6\" opacity=\".7\"/>`\nBut this is land across horizon perhaps. Then sea in front? Better horizon at y=410:\n`<path d=\"M0 410 Q ... L1000 420 L1000 500 L0 500Z\" fill=\"#89cfd4\"/>`\nDistant hills sit above sea (y390). Use muted teal. Add tiny white wave lines.\n\n```\n<path d=\"M0 390 C ... 1000 380 L1000 420 L0 420Z\" fill=\"#b8dcc9\"/>\n<rect y=420 height=90 fill=url(#sea)>\n<path wave lines...>\n<rect y=500 height=200 fill=url(#s","truncated":true}
